---
// The groupings door (owner directive, 2026-07-25). It used to be every industry stacked sector by
// sector with a count and a full column list each, four hundred lines tall, and the owner's
// complaint about it was exact: scrolling down and going back and forth. So it is now three things
// and nothing else.
//
//   1. The control bar, the same one every grouping table carries, so steering starts here and
//      continues from wherever the reader lands rather than sending him back.
//   2. One table, eleven rows, one per sector: how many industries, how many companies, and the
//      columns that sector's own table is read on. It sorts like any table on the site.
//   3. A dense alphabetical index of every industry table, bare links. This is the part that keeps
//      every table one hop from the door for a reader who already knows the name he wants, and for
//      a crawler. Shortening the page without it would push a hundred tables to two hops.
//
// What is deliberately NOT here: a sector-level table of medians. It would be arithmetic over filed
// figures and defensible on the letter of the house style, but a sortable grid of eleven sectors by
// median return is the closest thing to a ranking this site could print, and it would be read as
// one within a week. The table stays inventory.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GroupingPicker from "../../components/GroupingPicker.astro";
import { SECTOR_INDEX, ALL_INDUSTRIES, TOTAL_COMPANIES } from "../../lib/groupingsIndex.mjs";
import { dataAsOf, fmtAsOf } from "../../lib/freshness.mjs";

const refreshed = fmtAsOf(dataAsOf);
const industryCount = ALL_INDUSTRIES.length;
// Alphabetical, because this index exists for the reader who arrives with a name in mind. The table
// above it keeps the taxonomy's own order, which is a reading order and never a ranking.
const alphabetical = [...ALL_INDUSTRIES].sort((a, b) => a.noun.localeCompare(b.noun));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Groupings, Owner Scorecard"
  description={`The catalog arranged by what the businesses do: 11 sectors and ${industryCount} industries, each one comparative table of every member's filed figures in sortable columns.`}
>
  <header class="hero">
    <h1>The groupings</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The catalog arranged by what the businesses do. Start with a sector and read all of it in one
      table, then narrow to an industry when you want the lines that kind of business is really read
      on.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      A sector table shows the columns every member of the sector can answer, so companies that earn
      in different ways can still be read down the same column. An industry table shows more: what is
      contracted for next year in software, inventory days in semiconductors, deposits and the
      noninterest-bearing share in banks. Narrowing changes the instrument rather than filtering the
      list. {TOTAL_COMPANIES.toLocaleString()} records across the US, ADR and Japanese pools, in
      {" "}{industryCount} industries under 11 sectors. Which businesses a reader understands is the
      reader&rsquo;s own question, and the one thing this page does not answer.
    </p>
    {refreshed ? <p class="dateline">From the latest filings · data as of {refreshed}.</p> : null}
  </header>

  <GroupingPicker view="index" />

  <table class="sec" id="secTable">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th class="txt" data-t="s" data-key="sector" scope="col"><button type="button">Sector <span class="tri"></span></button></th>
        <th class="num" data-t="n" data-key="industries" scope="col"><button type="button">Industries <span class="tri"></span></button></th>
        <th class="num" data-t="n" data-key="companies" scope="col"><button type="button">Companies <span class="tri"></span></button></th>
        <th class="txt cols" scope="col"><span class="plainHead">The columns its table shows</span></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {SECTOR_INDEX.map((s) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="txt nm" data-s={s.name.toLowerCase()}><a href={s.href}>{s.name}</a></td>
          <td class="num" data-s={s.industries.length}>{s.industries.length}</td>
          <td class="num" data-s={s.count}>{s.count.toLocaleString()}</td>
          <td class="txt cols">{s.columns.map((c) => c.label).join(" · ")}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="tnote">
    Order is the taxonomy&rsquo;s own, a reading order and never a ranking. The counts are records the
    library holds, not a claim about how large a sector is.
  </p>

  <section class="alpha">
    <h2>Every industry table</h2>
    <p class="alphaNote">All {industryCount}, alphabetically, for when you already know the name.</p>
    <ul class="alphaList">
      {alphabetical.map((ind) => (
        <li><a href={`/groupings/${ind.slug}`}>{ind.noun}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="foot">
    Every figure on these tables is recomputed from the company&rsquo;s own SEC or EDINET filings, the
    same way as on its page. The columns name facts, weigh nothing, and form no rank.
  </p>

  <script is:inline>
    // The sector table sorts, the same act on the same terms as every other table here: plain DOM
    // re-ordering of rows already on the page, nothing fetched and nothing remembered. Eleven rows
    // in the taxonomy's own order until the reader asks for another.
    (function () {
      var table = document.getElementById("secTable");
      if (!table || !table.tHead || !table.tBodies.length) return;
      var ths = Array.prototype.slice.call(table.tHead.rows[0].cells);
      var tbody = table.tBodies[0];
      var cur = -1, dir = -1;
      ths.forEach(function (th, i) {
        var btn = th.querySelector("button");
        if (btn) btn.addEventListener("click", function () { sortBy(i); });
      });
      function sortBy(i) {
        var numeric = ths[i].getAttribute("data-t") === "n";
        dir = cur === i ? -dir : numeric ? -1 : 1;
        cur = i;
        var rows = Array.prototype.slice.call(tbody.rows);
        rows.sort(function (ra, rb) {
          var a = ra.cells[i].getAttribute("data-s"), b = rb.cells[i].getAttribute("data-s");
          if (a == null || b == null) return 0;
          if (numeric) return (parseFloat(a) - parseFloat(b)) * dir;
          return (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0) * dir;
        });
        for (var k = 0; k < rows.length; k++) tbody.appendChild(rows[k]);
        ths.forEach(function (th, k) {
          var tri = th.querySelector(".tri");
          if (tri) tri.textContent = k === i ? (dir === 1 ? "▲" : "▼") : "";
          if (k === i) th.setAttribute("aria-sort", dir === 1 ? "ascending" : "descending");
          else th.removeAttribute("aria-sort");
        });
      }
    })();
  </script>

  <style>
    .hero h1 { margin: 0 0 8px; }
    .lede { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 10px; max-width: 68ch; }
    .sub { color: var(--ink-dim); margin: 0; max-width: 72ch; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .dateline { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin: 10px 0 0 0; }

    /* The hero and the control bar want a little air between them without a rule doing the work
       twice; the bar carries its own bottom rule. */
    .hero { margin-bottom: 20px; }

    table.sec { width: 100%; border-collapse: separate; border-spacing: 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; margin: 4px 0 0; }
    table.sec th, table.sec td { padding: 8px 12px 8px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); text-align: left; vertical-align: baseline; }
    table.sec thead th { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--rule-strong); }
    table.sec th.num, table.sec td.num { text-align: right; padding-right: 22px; white-space: nowrap; }
    table.sec thead button, table.sec .plainHead { background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink); }
    table.sec thead button { cursor: pointer; }
    table.sec thead button:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    table.sec .plainHead { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 400; }
    table.sec thead .tri { display: inline-block; min-width: 1em; font-size: 9px; color: var(--accent); }
    table.sec td.nm a { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
    table.sec td.nm a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    table.sec td.num { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-dim); }
    table.sec td.cols { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.55; white-space: normal; }
    .tnote { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin: 10px 0 0; max-width: 78ch; }

    .alpha { margin: 30px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-top: 16px; }
    .alpha h2 { font-family: var(--serif); font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; }
    .alphaNote { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--muted); margin: 4px 0 10px; }
    .alphaList { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; columns: 3; column-gap: 28px; }
    .alphaList li { break-inside: avoid; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.85; }
    .alphaList a { color: var(--ink-dim); text-decoration: none; }
    .alphaList a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

    @media (max-width: 900px) { .alphaList { columns: 2; } }
    @media (max-width: 620px) {
      .alphaList { columns: 1; }
      table.sec td.cols, table.sec th.cols { display: none; }
    }

    .foot { font-family: var(--serif); font-style: italic; font-size: 13px; color: var(--muted); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); margin-top: 28px; padding-top: 12px; max-width: 78ch; line-height: 1.6; }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
